course: biology
lesson: 403
title: The real animals of the river bank: the water vole, the mole, the otter, the toad and the river birds
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «The Wind in the Willows» is about a Water Rat, a Mole, an Otter and a Toad who row boats, eat picnics and drive motor-cars. Under the jokes are real British animals, and Kenneth Grahame watched them closely on the River Thames.
explain: The Rat is not really a rat at all: he is a water vole, a chubby, blunt-nosed plant-eater that digs its burrow in the river bank and dives with a loud «plop». Today it is one of Britain's most endangered mammals.
explain: The Mole really does live underground in the dark, digs with huge spade-like paws and gets his food from tunnels; the Otter really is a strong swimmer who teaches its cubs to swim, just as Otter taught Portly at the ford.
explain: But real moles do not spring-clean with whitewash, real otters do not chase May-flies, and real toads walk slowly to their ponds instead of driving cars into them.
explain: So when you read, ask: which part is the real animal, and which part is the story?
fact: The Rat in the book is a water vole: it has a round blunt nose, small ears almost hidden in its fur and a short furry tail, while a brown rat has a pointed nose, big ears and a long scaly tail.
fact: Water voles eat grasses, reeds and other waterside plants, not fish, and they dig burrows in the bank with some entrances under the water, so they can slip away with a «plop».
fact: Water voles have vanished from most of the places in Britain where they once lived, mainly because American mink that escaped from fur farms hunt them in their burrows, and because river banks were cleared and walled.
fact: A European mole has velvety black fur that lies flat whichever way it is brushed, so it can move forwards or backwards in its tunnels without getting its fur rubbed the wrong way.
fact: Moles have tiny eyes that can tell light from dark but see very little; they find food by touch and smell with a pink, sensitive snout.
fact: A mole eats mostly earthworms that drop into its tunnels, and it may store live worms in a larder after biting their heads so they cannot crawl away; a molehill is just the soil pushed up from digging.
fact: Moles do not hibernate; they keep digging all winter, going deeper when the ground freezes, and they live alone except when they meet to breed in spring.
fact: An otter's home in the river bank is called a holt; otters mostly eat fish and eels, and also frogs and crayfish, and they mark their stretch of river with droppings called spraints.
fact: Otter cubs stay with their mother for about a year, and at first they are nervous of the water: the mother has to coax them in and teach them to swim and fish.
fact: Otters almost disappeared from England in the 1960s and 1970s, mostly because farm poisons got into the rivers and the fish; after those poisons were banned they came back, and they have been reported again in every county of England.
fact: Mayflies are eaten by trout and other fish that leap up at them, not by otters; an adult mayfly lives only a day or two and cannot even eat, after a year or more as a young nymph under the water.
fact: Ducks like mallards feed by tipping their heads under the water and their tails up to nibble plants on the bottom, which is the real «Up tails all!» of the Ducks' Ditty.
fact: A kingfisher dives from a branch to catch small fish and nests in a tunnel in the bank; a moorhen is a black water bird with a red beak; a dabchick is the little grebe, a small bird that dives for its food.
fact: A real common toad walks rather than hops, eats slugs, beetles and worms, and spends the winter asleep under logs or in compost heaps before walking back to its pond in spring.
exercise: What real animal is the book's Water Rat? | A water vole | A brown rat | A beaver | A guinea pig
exercise: How can you tell a water vole from a brown rat? | Blunt nose, hidden ears and a short furry tail | Pointed nose, big round ears and a long bare scaly tail | Webbed feet and a wide flat paddle of a tail | Long whiskers, a striped back and a bushy tail
exercise: What does a water vole mostly eat? | Grasses, reeds and waterside plants | Small fish caught in the shallows | Frogs, newts and tadpoles | Bread that people throw to the ducks
exercise: Which animal has been the biggest danger to water voles in Britain? | American mink escaped from fur farms | Herons standing in the shallow water | Swans and geese pulling up the reeds | Pike swimming up the burrows
exercise: Why can a mole move backwards easily in its tunnel? | Its fur lies flat in any direction | It has eyes at the back of its head | It walks backwards all its life | Its tunnels are wide enough to turn round in
exercise: How does a mole mostly find its food? | By touch and smell with its sensitive snout | By sight, with very large eyes that see in the dark | By listening for birds singing above | By following the smell of flowers
exercise: What is a molehill? | Soil pushed up from the mole's digging | The mole's nest, built above the ground | A pile of worms the mole has stored | A hill of earth piled up by ants
exercise: What do moles do in winter? | Keep digging, deeper when the ground is frozen | Sleep in a grass nest from October till April | Fly south to Africa with the swallows | Move into warm houses and barns till spring
exercise: What is an otter's home called? | A holt | A sett | A drey | A warren
exercise: What do otters mostly eat? | Fish and eels | Grass and reeds | Mayflies and other insects | Berries and nuts
exercise: How do otter cubs learn to swim? | Their mother coaxes them in and teaches them | They are born swimming and never need to learn | Their father throws them in the deep water | They watch the ducks and copy them
exercise: Why did otters almost vanish from England? | Farm poisons got into the rivers and the fish | They all swam away to the sea one hard winter | The rivers froze solid for many years in a row | Water voles chased them away
exercise: Which animals really leap at mayflies? | Trout and other fish | Otters and their cubs | Water voles at night | Moles in summer
exercise: How long does an adult mayfly live? | A day or two | About a year | About ten years | A whole summer
exercise: What are the ducks doing when it is «Up tails all»? | Tipping up to eat plants on the bottom | Sleeping with their heads under their wings | Looking for their lost ducklings | Washing their tail feathers in the river
exercise: Where does a kingfisher nest? | In a tunnel in the river bank | In a cup of mud under a barn roof | On a raft of floating reeds and sticks | High up in a hollow old oak tree
exercise: How does a common toad usually get about? | It walks | It hops long distances | It swims all day | It slides on its belly
test: «ratty's a rat innit, it's in his name» What is right? | He is a water vole: a blunt-nosed plant-eater with a short furry tail | He is a brown rat, the kind that lives in sewers, because the book says so | He is a rat that has learnt to swim from the Otter | He is a mouse that grew very big by living near water
test: Near a stream you hear a loud «plop» and see a chubby brown animal vanish under the bank. What was it most likely? | A water vole diving into its burrow | A big pike jumping right out of the water for a fly | A mole that slipped and fell in the river | A toad leaping from the bank into its pond
test: A cartoon mole wears dark glasses because it is «totally blind». Put it right. | A mole can tell light from dark but sees very little, and finds food by touch and smell | A mole sees perfectly well and only wears glasses in the story to look cool | A mole is blind from birth because the soil got into its eyes when it was a baby | A mole has no eyes at all, not even small ones, only two holes
test: «grandad dug up a mole's nest and found loads of worms with no heads, gross. why?» What is the best answer? | Moles bite worms' heads so they stay alive in a larder but cannot crawl off | The worms lost their heads in the cold winter frost, which bites off the soft parts | Worms grow without heads when they live too close to moles | Grandad's spade must have cut every single one of them in half
test: In the book the Mole spring-cleans his house with brooms and whitewash. What does a real mole do in spring? | Looks for a mate and digs new tunnels | Sweeps out its tunnels with its long tail | Paints the walls of its tunnels white with chalk | Moves into a house by the river
test: In the book the Otter dives after a May-fly at the picnic. What would a real otter more likely be chasing? | A fish or an eel | A dragonfly in the air | A sandwich from the basket | A duck's egg
test: «otters are extinct in england, my teacher said like 30 years ago» What is true now? | They nearly vanished, but came back and have been reported in every county | They are extinct in the wild and now only live in zoos and wildlife parks | They never lived in England at all, only in Scotland | They live only in the sea now, never in rivers
test: Portly «hasn't learnt to swim very well yet», and his father taught him at the ford. Which part is true of real otters? | Cubs are nervous of water and are taught by their mother | Otters are born strong swimmers and never need any lessons at all | Only the father otters teach the cubs, never the mothers | Otter cubs never swim at all until they are fully grown up
test: What would happen to water voles on a river if mink were trapped and the reeds allowed to grow back? | Water voles could come back and live there again | The voles would leave the river and go to live in the sea | The voles would all turn into brown rats within a few years | Nothing at all would change, as voles need no plants to eat
test: «toads hop like frogs, everyone knows that» Is that right for the common toad? | No, a common toad mostly walks with short legs | Yes, toads hop even further and higher than frogs do | Yes, but only when they are going backwards | No, toads crawl along on their bellies like snakes
test: ★★★ A survey team counts animals on 3 stretches of river: 4 water voles on each stretch, and 1 otter on 2 of them. A mink kills 2 voles. How many voles and otters are left in all? | 12 | 10 | 14 | 15
exam: Identify the animal which is represented, in the novel, by the character known as the Water Rat. | The water vole, a plant-eating rodent of the river bank | The brown rat, a rodent of towns, drains and farmyards | The beaver, which constructs dams of timber across rivers | The musk rat, introduced from North America
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the European mole. | It subsists principally upon earthworms, some of which it stores alive | It consumes chiefly the roots of grass and of garden flowers, which it gnaws in its tunnels | It feeds upon fish which it catches in streams at night | It eats only insects caught in the air
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the decline of the otter in England during the 1960s and 1970s. | Poisons used in farming entered the rivers and the fish | The flooding of every river in the land during the winter | Competition with the water vole for fish | The cutting down of the willows
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between the otter and the mayfly? | The otter feeds chiefly upon fish; it is trout that rise to take the mayfly | The otter consumes the mayfly as its principal food | The mayfly feeds upon young otters | The mayfly and the otter share a burrow
